The commands append values to a document, undo the last value, and redo the most recently undone value, discarding any redo history after a new append. Each kind of command affects the document and the history differently, and the output is the document's contents.

I model the document as a list that begins empty, and I keep a separate redo history as a list as well, which starts empty. When the command asks for a do operation with a value, I append that value to the document and then drop the entire redo history, since a new action wipes away any redo marker left by undo. When the command is an undo, I check that the document is non-empty, and if it is, I pop the last value off the document and push it onto the front of the redo history. Finally, when the command is a redo, I check that the redo history is not empty, and if it is not, I pop the first value off that history and append it to the end of the document.

None of the commands remove values they cannot, and I never write or read beyond the lengths of the two lists. The states evolve in the right order, and every last value moves cleanly between the two lists.

For an example with interplay, take the commands: do 1, do 2, undo, do 3, redo and redo. After the two appends the document is 1 then 2. Undo moves 2 to the history and leaves 1. Appending 3 clears that history and gives 1, 3. The first redo brings back 2 and the second redo again brings back 2 while the document becomes 1, 3, 2. The history is now empty again, so the state for the final command coincides with what the final assert expects.

