Each of the four numbers in the result is simply the minimum or maximum of one coordinate across all the points, so I can find them with two passes. For one pass I walk through every point and record the smallest x seen and the largest x seen, starting from the first point's x and then moving to the next as I go. A second pass does the same for the y coordinates. After both passes, I return the smallest x, smallest y, largest x and largest y in that order.

Passing through the list a second time is unavoidable without storing all the points, and it does not matter that the points are revisited. Because the list is guaranteed to be non-empty, something has to be recorded in both passes.

