The four corners of the inner rectangle must therefore lie within the outer one in both x and y directions. A corner lies within the outer rectangle when its x coordinate is at least outer's left edge and at most outer's right edge, and its y coordinate is within the corresponding vertical range. This covers overlapping corners.

So I work out the four corners of the inner rectangle and test each in turn. The first two corners are the lower left and lower right corners, followed by the upper left and upper right, where the lower corners keep the y coordinate of the inner rectangle's bottom edge and the upper ones its top edge. For every corner that passes, I keep a running count of how many have been accepted, and if that count reaches four, the inner rectangle is entirely within the outer one and the function returns True. If even one corner lies outside, the answer is False.

The comparison between outer's right edge and inner's right edge must be non-strict, so both rectangles share a right edge count as acceptable. Likewise for the top edge. Using outer's own values instead of a fixed maximum of ten makes the logic valid for any set of rectangles.

Checking four corners is more work than a single range comparison, but it handles every shape of inner rectangle, shared edges included, and it explicitly tracks whether the inner touches the outer rectangle rather than treating them as overlapping outright.

