def rectangle_contains(outer, inner):
    outer_left, outer_bottom, outer_right, outer_top = outer
    inner_left, inner_bottom, inner_right, inner_top = inner

    def point_inside(x, y):
        return outer_left <= x <= outer_right and outer_bottom <= y <= outer_top

    if point_inside(inner_left, inner_bottom):
        if point_inside(inner_right, inner_bottom):
            if point_inside(inner_left, inner_top):
                if point_inside(inner_right, inner_top):
                    return True
    return False
```

The four nested branches test each corner in turn, accepting the call only once all four coordinates have passed, and returning False on the first corner that appears outside. It evaluates precisely the four corners of the inner rectangle against the outer rectangle's sides.

Given a 2D grid of integers, with at least one row and consistently long rows, the requirement is to return, in left-to-right order, one entry for every column, that entry being the total of the integers in that column. An empty or rowless grid or one with empty rows should give an empty list.

The iterative approach walks the grid row by row and adds each element to a result list at the column index every element has. I used Python 3.7 dictionary insertion order, a feature that is stable in the CPython implementation wherever it is new and is implemented with an insertion-ordered container. Starting with an empty dictionary pairs column indices with running sums, then for every row every element is added to the sum at its column index, and at the very end the ordered dictionary's values are returned as a list. That order reproduction is only safe if every column index arrives the first time it is seen in left-to-right reading, which holds because the grid is not ragged and the outer loop reads from the first row to the last. If the grid is empty or has rows of zero length, the result is an empty list.

