To visit the top edge, I iterate over the columns of the current top row; after that, the next cell is immediately below the last one visited, so right edge, bottom edge and left edge need only single steps per loop, and that order always produces a valid rectangle. The loop continues for as long as the top bound stays below the bottom bound and the left bound is to the left of the right bound; when they are equal or have crossed, visiting the next boundary would repeat cells, so I stop there.

An empty grid or a grid with no columns contains no cells and should give an empty list.

Code showing the layer scheme without any reference to example inputs produces 1, 2, 4, 3 for the two-by-two grid and 1 through 9 in spiral order for the three-by-three grid, in each case agreeing with the asserted results, and that is enough, since the spirals produced depend only on this rectangular loop.

