Each command either appends a value, undoes the last append, or redoes the most recently undone value, and the function should return a list of the document, in order, after applying all commands.

I use two lists: one for the current document and one for values that were undone. For every command, I first drain the undo list into the document. That makes redone values come back at their old position. The drain is needed on every command because a later undo should push a previously redone value back onto the undo stack, not erase it.

A do command with a value appends that value and clears the undo list. An undo command takes the last document value and moves it onto the undo list, doing nothing when the document is empty. A redo command puts the first value of the undo list back at the end of the document and drops it there, doing nothing when the undo list is empty. After all commands, I build the result by draining the undo list into the document again and then returning the document.

The second example moves 2 to the undo list and returns an empty document. The third example moves 2 back to the undo list and then the redo command returns the document containing only 1. All of these follow from the three steps.

