Each command changes a document built from the do values and a redo history. A do command appends the value and clears the redo history. An undo removes the final value, putting it in front of the redo history, and does nothing when the document is empty. A redo re-appends the redo history's first value, and does nothing if the history is empty.

I therefore represent the state with two separate lists, the current document and the undo stack. For a do command I append the value to the document and empty the undo stack. For an undo I move the last entry of the document to the front of the undo stack, only if the document is non-empty. For a redo I move the first entry of the undo stack back into the document, only if the stack is non-empty.

This keeps the two states clean, with the document holding only the values still in use and the undo stack containing exactly the undone values in reverse order of undo.

Applying the three-sample commands shows it. After the two do commands containing one and two, the document holds one and two and the undo stack is empty. The undo moves two to the front of the stack, leaving only one. The redo then moves it back, and the document is back to one and two. That is precisely how the task's specification is encoded.

